                      {
                        "text": "Correct. The rate law can be experimentally determined by changing the initial concentration of each reactant and observing the change in the reaction rate. If the concentration of a reactant is increased and the rate of the reaction remains constant, the order of that reactant is zero. If the concentration of a reactant is tripled and the observed reaction rate increases by a factor of nine, then the order of the reaction (the exponent) for that reactant must be 2, since 3"
